      title: "Why This Course",
      body: `## Theater as a civic act

In fifth-century Athens, going to the theater was not a private entertainment. It was a **religious** festival in honor of the god **Dionysus**, a **civic** occasion organized by the city, a **competition** among poets and producers, and a **political** event, at which the citizens of the democracy gathered to watch stories about kings, gods, wars, and families. The plays were performed once, at a single festival, before an audience of thousands, and judged by citizens chosen by lot. A handful of them were later copied, studied, and performed again, and about forty-four survive complete. They include some of the greatest works of drama ever written.

This course reads them as works of art and as acts of a city. It complements **DRA 101, The Theater of Dionysus**, which reads the most famous tragedies, the *Oresteia*, *Oedipus the King*, *Antigone*, *Medea*, the *Trojan Women*, and the *Bacchae*, together with Aristotle's *Poetics*. To avoid repeating that course, CLT 110 reads **other** plays by the three tragedians, chosen to show the range of their art and its connection to the life of Athens, and gives a full lesson to **comedy**, which DRA 101 does not cover:

1. **The festival and the city** (this lesson): how the Dionysia worked, and what it meant.
2. **Aeschylus**: the *Persians*, the only surviving tragedy on a recent historical event, and *Prometheus Bound*.
3. **Sophocles**: *Ajax* and *Philoctetes*, two plays about the heroes of the Trojan War, and about honor, deception, and friendship.
4. **Euripides**: *Alcestis* and *Hippolytus*, two plays about marriage, desire, and death.
5. **Aristophanes**: *Clouds*, *Frogs*, and *Lysistrata*, and the world of Old Comedy.

Students who have taken DRA 101 will find that this course deepens their understanding of the plays they have read. Students who have not will find here a different way into Greek drama, through the festival and through plays that are less often read but no less remarkable.

## How to read the plays

Read the plays in a good modern translation. This course recommends the **University of Chicago Press Complete Greek Tragedies** (third edition, edited by Mark Griffith and Glenn Most, 2013), and for Aristophanes, the translations of Jeffrey Henderson or Alan Sommerstein. Because these translations are under copyright, this course quotes them only briefly, and gives its own plain renderings of the Greek.

Read the plays **aloud**, and try to imagine them **performed**: in daylight, in an open-air theater on the slope of the Acropolis, before fifteen thousand people, with masked actors, a chorus of singers and dancers, and music played on the double pipe (**aulos**). The texts we read are the scripts of a performance; much of what made them powerful, the music, the dance, the costumes, the spectacle, is lost. Good readers keep that loss in mind.

## The surviving plays

Of the hundreds of tragedies performed in Athens in the fifth century, only thirty-two survive complete, by three poets:

- **Aeschylus** (c. 525–456 BCE): seven plays, out of perhaps eighty;
- **Sophocles** (c. 497–406 BCE): seven plays, out of about one hundred and twenty;
- **Euripides** (c. 480–406 BCE): eighteen or nineteen plays, out of about ninety (the *Rhesus*, attributed to him, may be by another poet).

Of comedy, eleven plays by **Aristophanes** (c. 450–386 BCE) survive complete, the only complete works of **Old Comedy**. Of the later **New Comedy**, one nearly complete play by **Menander**, the *Dyskolos* ("The Grouch"), was recovered from an Egyptian papyrus published in 1958, along with large parts of several others.

Why did these plays survive, and not others? Partly by choice: in the fourth century, Athens decided to honor the three great tragedians with official texts, and later, in the Roman and Byzantine periods, teachers selected a small number of plays for school use. The seven plays of Aeschylus and of Sophocles that we have are those selections. Euripides is luckier: in addition to his selected plays, a manuscript survived containing a group of his plays in alphabetical order (from epsilon to kappa), which scholars call the "alphabetical plays," among them the *Helen*, the *Ion*, and the *Heracles*. Their survival shows us how much of a poet's work the school selections leave out.

## The fragments

Beyond the complete plays, we have thousands of **fragments**: quotations of lines and passages from lost plays, preserved in later authors, anthologies, and dictionaries, and, since the late nineteenth century, scraps of papyrus recovered from the rubbish heaps of Greco-Roman Egypt, especially at **Oxyrhynchus**. Some of these papyri preserve substantial parts of lost plays: large portions of Sophocles's satyr play *Trackers*, of Euripides's *Hypsipyle*, and of several comedies of Menander. New fragments are still being identified and published. The study of Greek drama is, in this sense, unfinished: every few years, a little more of the lost theater returns.

## The plays as scripts

Remember, too, that the texts we read have passed through many hands. Actors in the fourth century sometimes added lines or altered passages to suit their performances, so much so that the Athenian statesman **Lycurgus**, around 330 BCE, passed a law requiring that official copies of the plays of the three great tragedians be kept in the public archive, and that actors perform from them without changes. These official copies were later borrowed by the Library of Alexandria, which, according to a famous story, kept the originals and sent back copies, forfeiting the large deposit it had paid. Our texts descend, through the Alexandrian scholars and Byzantine copyists, from such editions; but scholars still argue about which lines are the poets' and which were added by actors.

> **Pause and consider.** Only about one play in ten by the three tragedians survives, mostly chosen by later teachers for schools. How might our picture of Greek tragedy be different if other plays had survived?`,
